Logs: CHARACTERS:

Mitochondrion: The mitochondrion is a bean-shaped organelle within the cell, responsible for energy production. [00:07-00:29]

Protein Complexes: Large, teal-colored protein structures embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, labeled as "complex I," "complex III," and "complex IV." [00:53-01:02]

Coenzyme Q: Small, mobile green carrier that moves within the inner membrane. [63s-75s]

Cytochrome c: Small green protein residing in the intermembrane space near Complex III. [63s-75s]

ATP Synthase: Large, blue protein complex embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, responsible for ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis. [75s-102s]

SCENES:

Scene 1: Translucent, spherical cell floating in a blue-gray fluid environment. [00:07-00:16]

Scene 2: Single, glowing blue mitochondrion. [00:17-00:29]

Scene 3: Cross-section of the inner mitochondrial membrane, displaying the electron transport chain. [63s-75s]

Scene 4: Horizontal phospholipid bilayer membrane with embedded protein complexes, illustrating ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis. [75s-102s]

EVENTS:

Event 1: Electron transport chain: NADH binds to Complex I, releasing an electron and a proton, which gets pumped into the intermembrane space. The electron travels through Complex I, reducing coenzyme Q to CoQH2, then to Complex III and to Cyt c, before being transferred to Complex IV, where electrons, protons, and oxygen converge to form water. [63s-75s]

Event 2: ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis: Protons accumulate in the upper compartment, creating an electrochemical gradient. ATP synthase rotates mechanically as protons flow through it, driving the catalytic conversion of ADP and inorganic phosphate into ATP. [75s-102s]

